@page "/curriculum"
@inject AcademyStore Store

<PageTitle>How courses are designed — Gov AI Academy</PageTitle>

<h1>How courses are designed</h1>
<p class="lede">
    The U.S. Department of Labor’s AI Literacy Framework (Training and Employment Notice No. 07-25) describes a baseline every worker should have.
    Approved courses in this academy start above that baseline. They teach role proficiency: applied judgment in a public-sector job, with an instructor in the class.
</p>
<p>
    Source: <a href="https://www.dol.gov/agencies/eta/advisories/ten-07-25">TEN 07-25</a>.
    The notes below are original. They are not a copy of the notice.
</p>

<AlertControl Severity="AlertSeverity.Info" Title="What “approved” means here">
    An approved course is instructor-led, practiced on the officer’s own tools and tasks, and closed out by attendance plus a required exercise checklist.
    Online live, in person, and hybrid all qualify. A self-paced recording does not.
</AlertControl>

<h2>Content areas, taught above the baseline</h2>
<AccordionControl Multiple="true">
    <AccordionItemControl Title="Understand AI Principles" InitiallyExpanded="true">
        Baseline literacy names ideas such as probabilistic output and invented facts. Approved classes use those ideas on a work product: a memo, a ticket group, or a table. Foundation courses do this without asking officers to train a model.
    </AccordionItemControl>
    <AccordionItemControl Title="Explore AI Uses">
        Uses are occupation-specific. A contract specialist looks at public-source market notes. A duty officer looks at alert grouping. The class does not tour a generic product menu.
    </AccordionItemControl>
    <AccordionItemControl Title="Direct AI Effectively">
        Prompt practice is tied to a template the office already has. Learners iterate, then keep a record of what they accepted.
    </AccordionItemControl>
    <AccordionItemControl Title="Evaluate AI Outputs">
        Every approved course includes a check against a source the learner can point to: a sheet, a log field, a cell, or a public page. The model is not the authority.
    </AccordionItemControl>
    <AccordionItemControl Title="Use AI Responsibly">
        Learners name what must not be pasted into a tool, who remains accountable, and which decisions stay with a person. The sample app itself refuses to be a place for real sensitive text.
    </AccordionItemControl>
</AccordionControl>

<h2>Delivery principles the approval gate requires</h2>
<ul class="fact-list">
    <li><strong>Experiential learning.</strong> Required exercises are hands-on. Reading about a tool is not enough.</li>
    <li><strong>Learning in context.</strong> The first meeting is where an agency swaps in its template, tool list, and redacted cases.</li>
    <li><strong>Human skills.</strong> Classes pair the draft with judgment, plain language, and domain knowledge.</li>
    <li><strong>Prerequisites.</strong> Each approved course states the digital and job skills a learner needs so the class does not assume a programmer.</li>
    <li><strong>Pathways.</strong> Foundation work leads to practitioner and technical evaluation courses. The path is role proficiency, not a single orientation.</li>
    <li><strong>Enabling roles.</strong> Supervisors have a course on coaching a team. Cyber and oversight courses also practice review of someone else’s output.</li>
    <li><strong>Agility.</strong> Modules are short enough for a host to replace the scenario when tools or policy change, without rewriting the attendance rule.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Catalog against the gate</h2>
<p>Select a row to read the course. “Gaps” is zero only when the course is eligible for approval.</p>
<GridControl TValue="DesignRow" DataSource="_rows" AllowSorting="true" AllowFiltering="true"
             AllowSelection="true" Height="420px" EnableHover="true">
    <GridColumnsBase>
        <GridColumn Field="@nameof(DesignRow.Title)" HeaderText="Course" Width="280px" />
        <GridColumn Field="@nameof(DesignRow.Level)" HeaderText="Level" Width="160px" />
        <GridColumn Field="@nameof(DesignRow.Role)" HeaderText="Role path" Width="170px" />
        <GridColumn Field="@nameof(DesignRow.Approval)" HeaderText="Status" Width="110px" />
        <GridColumn Field="@nameof(DesignRow.Gaps)" HeaderText="Rule gaps" Width="110px" Type="ColumnType.Number" TextAlign="TextAlign.Right" />
        <GridColumn Field="@nameof(DesignRow.Areas)" HeaderText="Content areas" Width="280px" />
    </GridColumnsBase>
</GridControl>

<h2>Level tracks and role paths</h2>
<div class="card-grid">
    <CardControl Title="Foundation" Subtitle="Non-technical" Elevation="1">
        <p>Plain-language practice for officers who write, coordinate, or brief. Still above a one-time literacy talk.</p>
    </CardControl>
    <CardControl Title="Practitioner" Subtitle="Role depth" Elevation="1">
        <p>Policy, acquisition, data review, and supervision. The exercise matches the file or the team the officer already has.</p>
    </CardControl>
    <CardControl Title="Advanced / technical" Subtitle="Evaluation and operations" Elevation="1">
        <p>Sampling, logging, and confirmation. Not a class on building exploits or training a production model.</p>
    </CardControl>
</div>

@code {
    private sealed class DesignRow
    {
        public string Title { get; set; } = "";
        public string Level { get; set; } = "";
        public string Role { get; set; } = "";
        public string Approval { get; set; } = "";
        public int Gaps { get; set; }
        public string Areas { get; set; } = "";
    }

    private List<DesignRow> _rows = [];

    protected override void OnInitialized()
    {
        _rows = Store.Courses.Select(c => new DesignRow
        {
            Title = c.Title,
            Level = AcademyLabels.Level(c.Level),
            Role = AcademyLabels.Role(c.Role),
            Approval = AcademyLabels.Approval(c.Approval),
            Gaps = PedagogyRules.Violations(c).Count,
            Areas = string.Join(", ", c.DolContentAreas)
        }).ToList();
    }
}
